  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nOut west, a deepening trough over the Great Basin should finally \npush this stubborn ridging east of our region beginning Sunday. \nTemperatures should be slightly cooler starting Sunday as an \nincrease in cloud cover due to southwesterly flow aloft and \nincreased surface moisture as low level flow picks up out of the \nsoutheast. By Monday, this trough will eject quickly out over the \nHigh Plains, with a separate piece of energy lagging behind over \nnorth Texas Monday into early Tuesday. This shortwave should provide \njust enough lift to pop off some showers and perhaps a couple of \nthunderstorms primarily Tuesday morning through the afternoon.\n\nBeyond Tuesday, both the ECMWF and GFS, as well as their AI suites \nhave advertised a weak frontal boundary working into the region \nWednesday, but it is unlikely to have much impact on temperatures. \nWhat may actually have bigger implications for our weather looks to \narrive by late next week and Halloween weekend. Medium range \nensembles have continued to hone in on the potential for a stronger \ncold front either next Friday or Saturday as well as the potential \nfor moisture from what is now Hurricane Vance to get dragged \nnorthward into south Texas.",
